Serial port: poll the host once every 16 idle asks and send in frame-sized writes; MIDI SysEx
buffers handed back lazily instead of slept for

Measured on the emulation thread with a TCP serial target and nothing arriving: the UART calls
readseravail()/readser() once per byte period - every scanline at the fast rates, ~15 kHz - and
each call went to accept()/recv() (or ClearCommError()+ReadFile() for a COM device) to be told
nothing was there. On the way out writeser() flushed after every byte, so a printed line was one
send() and one TCP segment per character.

- An empty rx_buf is refilled once every RX_IDLE_POLL_EVERY (16) asks. A read that finds data
  lets the very next empty ask go straight to the source, so a stream longer than the buffer
  keeps flowing at full rate; adm_hostio_poll() reads ahead once per frame besides, for a COM
  device now as well as a socket.
- writeser() only buffers. writeser_flush(), which the UART calls once per byte period, sends when
  TX_FLUSH_AT (256) bytes are waiting; anything less goes out with adm_hostio_poll() at the end of
  the frame, or in closeser() so a port change never loses the tail. checkserwrite() flushes only
  when the buffer could not otherwise take the bytes.
- midi_send_sysex() prepared a MIDIHDR, sent it, then spun on midiOutUnprepareHeader() with
  Sleep(1) for up to 100 ms per message - on the emulation thread, per SysEx, so a patch dump
  stalled the machine for frames at a time. Four prepared buffers now rotate: a message takes a
  free one and returns at once, and a buffer comes back when a later message finds MHDR_DONE on
  it, or at midi_close() after midiOutReset() has returned them all. With all four in flight the
  message is dropped and logged once rather than waited for.

Applies to libretro-uae 2b0ed42fe565fb997a0627aaa8f44e0948b527f8 in the order given by
patches/series.txt.

